issue.context:
Found by the Opus council seat, 2026-07-19, and verified in code.

server.rs:3300-3310, in the attach forwarder:

    // Bounded channel (itr#82): drop a frame on 'Full'
    // (slow/stalled TUI) rather than block this forwarder
    // or grow memory; only a closed channel ends it.
    match tx.try_send(msg) {
        Ok(()) => {}
        Err(TrySendError::Full(_)) => {
            warn!(%sess_id, "TUI conn channel full; dropping terminal frame");
        }
        Err(TrySendError::Closed(_)) => { break; }
    }

The Full arm falls through and continues the loop. CONN_CHANNEL_CAPACITY = 1024 (server.rs:25). A client whose channel is momentarily full loses that output frame PERMANENTLY — not a recoverable broadcast Lagged, not a gap marker on the wire, just a warn line and a hole in the middle of the stream.

WHY THIS MATTERS BEYOND ITSELF: ADR-0012 asserts, and itr#626's two fix rounds (e3d29b9, dc63cad) laboured to establish, that attach delivers every output frame EXACTLY ONCE. That property is false on the ordinary steady-state path regardless of the attach seam, because this arm discards frames by design. Two rounds of increasingly precise seam atomicity were performed upstream of a channel that drops. 483 daemon tests, clippy and 24/24 e2e all pass with this in place.

THIS IS ARGUABLY NOT A BUG BUT AN UNRECONCILED CONTRADICTION between two decisions: itr#82 deliberately chose availability (never block the forwarder, never grow memory unboundedly) over exactly-once; ADR-0012 later asserted exactly-once. Both cannot hold. The resolution is a product decision, but the SILENCE is indefensible either way — the client cannot distinguish 'you saw everything' from 'you are missing an unknown amount', which is exactly the class of failure that made itr#624 read as a broken scroll wheel.

Note the same pattern appears at server.rs:5973 (rejected_full counter) — that path at least counts.
